# Trivy ignore file (job 6 — "Trivy — fail on HIGH/CRITICAL", scoped like
# scripts/ci/audit-high.sh's npm-audit allowlist). Every entry is a named,
# reviewed classification, not a blanket suppression — Trivy still fails on any
# HIGH/CRITICAL not listed here.
#
# tar-fs — pulled ONLY by @puppeteer/browsers, which uses it to extract the
# Chromium archive that puppeteer downloads on install. This project sets
# PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD=true (CI env + Docker build) and drives a
# system/preinstalled Chromium via PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH (prod: the Alpine
# apk chromium; CI job 4: the runner's google-chrome). So @puppeteer/browsers'
# download+extract path — the only caller of tar-fs — never runs: tar-fs is
# dead code at runtime, not reachable from any request. These are the SAME
# tar-fs advisories already accepted in scripts/ci/audit-high.sh's npm-audit
# allowlist; this makes the image scanner consistent with that reviewed
# decision. package.json pins `overrides.tar-fs: ^3.1.3` (the fixed line), which
# materialises the patched tar-fs on the next clean lockfile regeneration — at
# which point these lines become redundant and should be removed.
CVE-2024-12905
CVE-2025-48387
CVE-2025-59343
#
# ws — pulled by puppeteer as its Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) client. It
# connects ONLY to the local Chromium child process's debug port over loopback
# (never binds/listens, never faces the network or any request), so these
# advisories are not reachable in this topology. Same advisories as the
# audit-high.sh npm-audit allowlist; overrides.ws pins the fixed line for the
# next clean lockfile regen.
CVE-2024-37890
CVE-2026-48779
